WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.050 --> 00:00:01.988
Here's the matrix in one view.

2
00:00:01.988 --> 00:00:06.826
The sponsor and CIO decide the platform
and when the old services retire.

3
00:00:06.826 --> 00:00:10.513
The CISO decides encryption,
DNSSEC and logging.

4
00:00:10.513 --> 00:00:13.664
The DNS and DDI team
decides the namespace,

5
00:00:13.664 --> 00:00:17.537
the edge target, the forwarding
and the address plan.

6
00:00:17.537 --> 00:00:22.950
Network engineering and AD administrators
do much of the work, but don't decide it.

7
00:00:22.950 --> 00:00:25.725
Why does the authorizing
official appear twice?

8
00:00:25.725 --> 00:00:29.650
Because many DNS changes are
risk decisions in disguise.

9
00:00:29.650 --> 00:00:34.387
Pointing an edge at a public resolver
quietly turns off Protective DNS.

10
00:00:34.387 --> 00:00:38.389
So every standing exception goes
to the authorizing official,

11
00:00:38.389 --> 00:00:40.313
with an owner and a review date.

12
00:00:40.313 --> 00:00:42.380
Next, everyday changes.
